id: pm-cpzt82
title: "The project shipped 68 releases and none of them exists in its own relationship graph, so the timeline the record is supposed to explain is reachable only by grouping a scalar"
description: "Measured 2026-08-11 across all 2,437 items.\n\n1,782 items carry a release value and 68 distinct release identities appear. The values are totally ordered by construction, because this project's versions are date-based. Every item that shipped therefore knows which epoch carried it, and the epochs know their own order.\n\nNone of that is in the graph.\n\nrelease is a scalar string on an item. The schema declares 17 item types and not one of them denotes a release, an epoch, or a shipped increment. The graph holds ten edge kinds and not one of them relates an item to the release that carried it, or one release to the release before it.\n\nEvery consequence is a traversal question the record cannot answer:\n\nwhat shipped in v2026.8.9 is a group-by over a string field, not a neighbourhood, so it costs a full-corpus scan and returns no structure;\n\nwhich release preceded v2026.8.9 requires parsing the version identifier, and that parse is the only ordering the project has;\n\nwhich release introduced the defect an item fixes has no path at all, because the fixing item and the introducing item are joined only through prose;\n\nwhat a release inherited as open blockers from its predecessor cannot be computed, because the boundary between two epochs is not a node a traversal can stop at.\n\nThe gap is visible in the project's own exemplars. The bundled vcs-exemplar extension registers Changeset and VcsRef item types, so modelling a version-control epoch as a first-class node is demonstrated for a foreign domain and left unused for this project's own releases.\n\nThis is the missing dimension for every consumer that wants to read the record as a history rather than as a set. Point-in-time projection, evolution traversal, throughput forecasting and the changelog all currently derive the epoch from a mutable timestamp or from a string, which is also why release attribution and changelog ordering keep drifting.\n\nThe epoch representation must stay derived. A hand-maintained release ledger would be a fourth ledger to disagree with the three the project already keeps."
type: Issue
status: open
priority: 1
tags[5]: governance,graph,history,release,timeline
created_at: "2026-08-11T13:51:47.918Z"
updated_at: "2026-08-11T13:51:47.918Z"
author: "harness:claude-code"
estimated_minutes: 300
acceptance_criteria: "A release epoch is representable as a first-class node with a declared type, so epoch membership and epoch order are edges rather than a scalar plus a version parse; Every item carrying a release value is joined to its epoch by a typed edge, and the join is derived from the recorded value rather than asserted item by item; Consecutive epochs carry a typed ordering edge, so a traversal reconstructs the full release sequence without interpreting version identifiers; Traversal answers what shipped in a named release and which release preceded it, and both are bounded reads rather than full-corpus group-bys; Items with no release value appear as a named residual population rather than being silently excluded, so a shipped but unstamped item is visible; The epoch representation is derived and reproducible from the immutable record alone, so it never becomes a fourth release ledger to disagree with the three that already exist"
parent: pm-96h7
risk: medium
confidence: 90
